module product.p4261.e1_photographs

// THE E1'S PHOTOGRAPH STORE: the frames the 2026-11-02/03 atlas holds, each identified by the SHA-256
// of its bytes. Its current source is the NAMED atlas snapshot p4261-e1-b 2026-11-03b, schema 2, supersedes 2026-11-03: an index file beside the
// receipts says which of them are current, and the generator reads nothing that index does not name by role.
// Discovery is not allowed -- a recursive glob or a newest-file rule would re-admit the superseded
// inventories, whose cleanup stripped underscores and silently merged U1_2 with U12, U2_2 with U22 and U3_2
// with U32. A reference designator's exact spelling is its identity; normalisation may make a search key and
// may never make identity.
//
// Generated by
// docs/plans/2026-11-03-p4261-e1-atlas/scripts/gen_e1_photographs_dag.py (private branch
// p4261/e1-atlas-handoff) from that folder's manifest_e1_all_photos.csv, the two photo registries, and
// batch 5's manifest_e1_sd_photos_2026-11-03.csv with photo_registry_sd_2026-11-03.csv, so a claim about
// this carrier cites an artefact rather than a sentence: a marking row and a store row are about the same
// frame because their digests match, whatever either is called in a folder.
//
// A row's byte count is the manifest's, carried as std.measure's existing `FiniteByteSize` -- the byte
// size whose count is a `PositiveMeasureCount`, so a store row that claims a file exists cannot state a
// zero-length frame. Minting a second positive byte carrier beside it would be a failed decomposition
// (gunbc.design_argument::minimize_redundancy). The number a reader compares against the file they open is the manifest's own, so
// `predecessor` is one less than it.
//
// Rows are named for the atlas photo id the registries use (the inventories' `photos` column), or for what
// the file is called where no registry lists it; batch 5's rows are named `e1_sd_photo_<photo id>`. The name
// is for people; `content` is what resolves.
//
// BATCH 5, THE SD-CARD FRAMES (110 of these rows). The Andonstar wrote them itself, from its own sensor, to
// its SD card: `AndonstarSdCardSensor`, not the screen capture's value, because a sensor frame and a picture
// of a screen are different grains and a claim about legibility must say which it read. Their day is
// 2026-11-03, the day the operator says the bench shot them: the microscope's clock is wrong and the file
// names and EXIF say 2020-01-12, so the date on these rows comes from the operator and NEVER from the file.
// Their side comes from the registry; the seven frames in E1/photos_sd_unplaced have no established side, so
// they carry `SurfaceUnrecorded` with what would close it -- they stay citable, and they enter no per-side
// count, because a part placed from one would be sitting on a guessed side of the board.
//
// The capture note in the registry names the device for 22 frames and is silent for the rest, so those rows
// carry `DeviceUnrecorded` with the note that would close it; the same holds for the capture date on frames
// the registry lists as earlier shots. Nothing here invents a device or a day.
//
// A generated image (grid overviews, zone drafts, the U10_P2 pinout, the power-path diagram) is a row too,
// typed `GeneratedImage`: it can locate a reading and cannot ground one, which
// product.electrical.photograph's `citation_verdict` enforces. An image we drew cannot corroborate the
// readings it was drawn from.

import std.content_hash { Sha256Digest, Sha256DigestHex }
import std.measure { finite_byte_size, positive_measure_count }
import std.types { List, NonEmptyStr }
import product.electrical.photograph {
  BottomSurface,
  AndonstarMicroscopeScreen,
  CapturedFrame,
  CapturedOn,
  CaptureDateUnrecorded,
  DeviceUnrecorded,
  GeneratedImage,
  PhoneCamera,
  AndonstarSdCardSensor,
  SurfaceUnrecorded,
  Photograph,
  StoreLocation,
  TopSurface,
  photograph_in_store,
}
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_asset_identity { p4261_e1_b }

